{
  "systemPrompt": "Role: Assume you are a senior HR professional with 10+ years of experience hiring software engineers.",
  "userPrompt": "Task: Write a short, high-impact cover letter that captures attention quickly and is easy to skim, while remaining professional and credible.\nThe letter should feel clearly written for this specific company and role, not reusable or generic.\n\nPrimary intent: This cover letter should function as an extended hook — engaging enough to be read in full, without feeling dense or generic.\n\nTone & voice:\n• First-person (\"I\"), natural and authentic\n• Professional, calm, and lightly conversational\n• Confident without being promotional or sales-driven\n• Sounds like it was written by the candidate himself, not by HR or AI\n• Subtle personality is encouraged; humour and exaggeration are not\n\nReadability rules (strict):\n• Short paragraphs only (1–3 sentences each)\n• No long or information-heavy sentences\n• Do not stack multiple technical concepts in a single sentence\n• White space is intentional; prioritise ease of reading over completeness\n\nContent rules:\n• Open with a role- or company-specific observation that shows familiarity with the team's work, product, or problem space\n• CRITICAL: Directly address specific requirements, technologies, or challenges mentioned in the job description\n• Explicitly connect the candidate's experience to what the JD is looking for\n• Use the CV hook summary as the factual anchor, but do not repeat it verbatim\n• Base all content strictly on the candidate's documented experience\n• Do not introduce new skills, tools, or achievements\n• Avoid buzzwords, clichés, and motivational language\n• Show clear alignment: demonstrate how specific past work matches specific JD requirements\n\nStructure:\nParagraph 1 (1–2 sentences):\n• A concise, non-generic opening that references something specific about the company, team, or role\n• Natural positioning of the candidate without self-promotion\n\nParagraph 2 (2–3 sentences):\n• One or two concrete examples of relevant technical or delivery experience that DIRECTLY match JD requirements\n• Explicitly connect the example to specific JD needs (e.g., \"This aligns with your need for...\", \"This experience maps to your requirement for...\")\n• Focus on real contribution and context, not breadth\n• Keep language plain and readable\n• Show clear matching: make it obvious why this experience is relevant to THIS role\n\nParagraph 3 (1–2 sentences):\n• A calm close that signals readiness for next steps or further discussion\n• Create forward momentum without using phrases like \"excited,\" \"passionate,\" or \"keen\"\n• Emphasise contribution and alignment rather than aspiration\n\nLength:\n• 120–150 words total\n• Optimised for skim-reading and completion\n\nJob Description:\n{{jd}}\n\nHiring Pain Points:\n{{painPoints}}\n\nCV Hook Summary:\n{{summary}}\n\nExperience Bullets:\n{{experienceBullets}}\n\nMapping:\n{{mapping}}\n\nCandidate Experience:\n{{candidateExperience}}\n\nWrite your response in plain text format. Do NOT use JSON format. Simply write the cover letter directly.",
  "temperature": 0.8,
  "maxTokens": 800
}
